From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 12:49:40 +0200
Subject: MIPS: intel-mips: reach the hazard barrier by a PC-relative branch

platform_eva_init programs the segmentation registers and then needs an
instruction hazard barrier twice: once before reading CP0 SegCtl back to
set the K bit, and once at the end. It got both by calling out to
mips_ihb with "jal", an absolute jump that encodes the low 28 bits of
the target's link-time address and takes the top four from the PC. So it
depends on mips_ihb having been linked into the same 256 MB region as
the code doing the jump, and on the address the linker chose being the
address the CPU is actually running under.

That is a bad thing to depend on here. smp_slave_setup expands this same
macro on a secondary VPE arriving from the VMB firmware, before EVA is
programmed, under whatever segmentation the firmware happened to leave. The
whole point of the macro is that the address environment is not yet the one
the kernel was linked for.

A vendor GPL release of Linux 4.9.337 removes the dependency: the jal
sites become "bal" to a macro-local trampoline that inlines what
mips_ihb is (arch/mips/include/asm/mach-lantiq/kernel-entry-init.h). bal
is PC-relative with a 16-bit offset, so it resolves against where the
code is really executing, and the trampoline is a handful of
instructions away. mips_ihb's body is exactly ".set MIPS_ISA_LEVEL_RAW;
jr.hb ra; nop" (arch/mips/kernel/entry.S:179), so inlining it is not an
approximation. A "b" over the trampoline keeps the fallthrough right.

Both boards boot four VPEs today and the jal resolves correctly, so this
fixes no observed failure. It removes a latent address dependency from the
one place in the kernel that cannot assume its addresses.

Two details are specific to how our copy of the macro is shaped.

The label names use the \@ macro-invocation counter, because this macro
is expanded twice in the same file -- from kernel_entry_setup at
arch/mips/kernel/head.S:88 and from smp_slave_setup at :152 -- and fixed
names would be a duplicate-symbol error. Assembling the construct
standalone confirms it: the two expansions emit platform_eva_init_1_*
and platform_eva_init_3_*.

The trampoline is placed inside the macro's existing ".set push / .set
reorder" region rather than after the ".set pop", which is where the
vendor puts it. head.S is under ".set noreorder" at file scope, so past
the pop the assembler would stop filling delay slots and the "b" over
the trampoline would take "jr.hb ra" as its own delay-slot instruction.
Inside the region the assembler fills both branches with nops, and
push/pop also scopes the MIPS_ISA_LEVEL_RAW that jr.hb needs instead of
leaking it to the rest of the file. The vendor's four-variant macro has
a different shape around the pop; ours has a single variant, so only two
bal sites apply.

There is a third expansion site, outside head.S and easy to miss:
arch/mips/kernel/cps-vec.S:129 calls eva_init, which under CONFIG_EVA is
nothing but platform_eva_init (asm/eva.h:30-32). Both changes hold there
for the same reasons rather than by luck. \@ counts per assembler run,
so cps-vec.S numbers its own expansion independently of head.S's; the
labels are never .globl, so they are STB_LOCAL and cannot collide across
object files at link time; and the trampoline lives inside the macro's
own ".set push ... .set reorder ... .set pop" region, so it neither
inherits nor disturbs the reorder state of whichever file expands it.
That last point is what makes the placement argument above a property of
the macro rather than a head.S-specific accident.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/include/asm/mach-intel-mips/kernel-entry-init.h b/arch/mips/include/asm/mach-intel-mips/kernel-entry-init.h
index b91b9186a2d9..3e0ef5ec2587 100644
--- a/arch/mips/include/asm/mach-intel-mips/kernel-entry-init.h
+++ b/arch/mips/include/asm/mach-intel-mips/kernel-entry-init.h
@@ -78,13 +78,25 @@
 	or      t0, t2
 	mtc0    t0, $5, 4
 
-	jal     mips_ihb
+	bal     platform_eva_init_\@_mips_ihb
 	mfc0    t0, $16, 5
 	li      t2, 0x40000000      /* K bit */
 	or      t0, t0, t2
 	mtc0    t0, $16, 5
 	sync
-	jal     mips_ihb
+	bal     platform_eva_init_\@_mips_ihb
+
+	b       platform_eva_init_\@_end
+	/*
+	 * mips_ihb inlined as a PC-relative trampoline. The \@ macro
+	 * invocation counter keeps the labels distinct: this macro is
+	 * expanded twice, from kernel_entry_setup and from smp_slave_setup.
+	 */
+platform_eva_init_\@_mips_ihb:
+	.set    MIPS_ISA_LEVEL_RAW
+	jr.hb   ra
+	nop
+platform_eva_init_\@_end:
 
 	.set    pop
 	.endm
